[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .   # -> c_src/build/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation && cargo build --release                                 # -> translation/target/release/driver
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cd translation && cargo test                                            # differential suite
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Result
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **No output mismatches were found.** Every enumerated input class, plus a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2,200-case randomized/boundary sweep, produced identical stdout, stderr and exit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m status from both binaries.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The sections below record the branch inventory that was enumerated and, for each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-obvious behavior, the divergence a straightforward translation *would* have
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m introduced and how the Rust code avoids it. These are the places to re-check if
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the Rust program is ever modified.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Branch inventory of the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `fma_array(out, mul1, mul2, add, len)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Single loop: `out[i] = mul1[i] * mul2[i] + add[i]`, signed `int` arithmetic.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `call_fma(data, len)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `if (len == 0) return 0;` — early return, the only guarded path.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Otherwise allocates three VLAs of length `len`, fills `ones`/`zeros`, calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `fma_array` with `mul1 = ones`, `mul2 = data`, `add = zeros`, and returns
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `out[len-1]`. Net effect: the function returns `data[len-1]`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `for (i = 0; i < 100; i++)` — bounded at 100 items.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `if (scanf("%d", &data[i]) != 1) break;` — breaks on both an input failure
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (EOF, `scanf` returns `EOF`) and a matching failure (`scanf` returns `0`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `printf("%d\n", result)` — exactly one line, then `return 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So the observable contract is: *print the last value successfully parsed by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%d")` from at most the first 100 items, or `0` if none was parsed.*
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Exit status is always 0 and stderr is always empty.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that had to be preserved (verified, not "fixed")
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `scanf` reads across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf("%d")` skips any run of C whitespace (` `, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the number, so line structure is irrelevant. A translation built on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `read_line`/`BufRead::lines` and per-line parsing would diverge on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"1\n2\n3"`, `"1\r\n2\r\n3"`, `"  \n\t 1 \x0b 2 \x0c 3 "`, and on inputs where a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m single line holds several numbers.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust side implements a byte-level `scanf("%d")` (`Stream` + `scanf_d`) with a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m one-byte pushback, mirroring `getc`/`ungetc`, rather than any line-based reader.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. A matching failure stops the loop; it is not an error and not skipped
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `"1 2 x 3"` prints `2`, not `3` and not an error. `scanf` returns 0, `break`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fires, and the trailing `3` is never read. Two plausible wrong behaviors:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m skipping the bad token and continuing (would print `3`), or treating it as an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m error (would write to stderr and/or exit non-zero). Neither happens: the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program always exits 0 with empty stderr.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Sub-cases verified: junk as the very first token (`"abc"` → `0`), junk glued to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m digits (`"1 2 3x4"` → `3`), a float (`"1.5"` → `1`, the `.5` stops the scan),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exponent notation (`"1e9"` → `1`), `0x` prefixes (`"0x10"` → `0`, since `%d` is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m base 10 and stops at `x`), and comma/semicolon separators.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Sign handling, including the malformed cases
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` accepts one optional `+`/`-` immediately before the digits. A sign not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m followed by a digit is a matching failure with the offending character pushed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m back — so `"-"`, `"+"`, `"-x"`, `"--5"`, `"- 5"` all yield `0`. `"5-"` yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `5` (the first conversion succeeds, the second fails).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Out-of-range values: `long` saturation, then truncation to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is the subtlest part. glibc converts `%d` through a `long`-width
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtol`-style accumulation, saturates at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on overflow, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then truncates the result to `int` on assignment. Observed and matched:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | output | why |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` (`INT_MAX+1`) | `-2147483648` | fits in `long`, truncated to `int` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` (`2^32`) | `0` | low 32 bits are zero |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967295` (`UINT_MAX`) | `-1` | |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-2147483649` | `2147483647` | |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` (`LONG_MAX`) | `-1` | low 32 bits all ones |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775808` (`LONG_MAX+1`) | `-1` | saturates to `LONG_MAX`, then truncates |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775809` | `0` | saturates to `LONG_MIN`, low 32 bits zero |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `"9"×40` | `-1` | saturates to `LONG_MAX` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `"-9"×40` | `0` | saturates to `LONG_MIN` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `"0"×1000` | `0` | zeros never overflow, regardless of length |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation using `i32::from_str` would error on all of these and print `0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instead; one using `i64::from_str` would error (rather than saturate) past
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `LONG_MAX`. `scanf_d` accumulates into `i64`, sets a `saturated` flag on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `checked_mul`/`checked_add` failure while still consuming the remaining digits,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m clamps to `i64::MIN`/`i64::MAX`, and only then casts to `i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified against a sweep of `10^e ± {0,1,2}` for `e` in `1..40` and of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `2^31`, `2^32`, `2^63`, `2^64` (and their `-1` neighbours) with `+`, `-`, and no
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sign — all identical.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. Signed `int` arithmetic in `fma_array`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `ones[i] * data[i] + zeros[i]` is `1 * x + 0`, which cannot overflow, but the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust code uses `wrapping_mul`/`wrapping_add` so it can never panic in debug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m builds even if the multiplicands change. A plain `*`/`+` would be an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m overflow panic in a debug build — a behavior C does not have.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. The 100-item bound
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The array holds exactly 100 `int`s and the loop condition stops there. With 101
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m or more numbers on stdin, only the first 100 are consumed, `data[99]` is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m answer, the remaining bytes are left unread, and the exit status is still 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified at 0, 1, 2, 98, 99, 100, 101, 150 and 200 items, and with junk placed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m after the 100th value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. Uninitialized `data[100]` is never observable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `int data[100]` is uninitialized in C, but `call_fma` only reads `data[0..len]`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m where `len == i` is the count actually written, and returns immediately when
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `len == 0`. The Rust version zero-initializes its array; because the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m never reads an uninitialized element, this is not observable. (Had `call_fma`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m lacked the `len == 0` guard, `out[len-1]` with `len == 0` would be an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m out-of-bounds read and there would be nothing well-defined to match.)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. Output shape and exit status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Exactly `"%d\n"` on stdout — a single line with a trailing newline, no leading
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m or trailing spaces, no other output. stderr is always empty and the exit status
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is always 0, on every input class including pure junk and empty input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Non-stdin edge cases also checked (all identical)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdin closed (`0<&-`), stdin pointed at a directory → both print `0`, exit 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - stdout closed (`>&-`), stdout to an already-closed pipe, stdout to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `/dev/full` → both exit 0 with no stderr. (Rust's default `SIGPIPE` handling
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and its ignored write errors happen to agree with glibc here, which silently
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   discards the failed flush at exit.)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Non-UTF-8 and binary stdin: embedded `NUL`, bytes `\xff`/`\xfe`, a truncated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   UTF-8 sequence, and a stream of all 256 byte values. A translation that read
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   stdin through `String`/`read_to_string` would fail on these; `Stream` works on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   raw bytes.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Test coverage map
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/differential.rs`, 19 tests, none `#[ignore]`d:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | test | branch / input class |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `empty_input` | `call_fma` `len == 0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `whitespace_only_inputs` | EOF reached while skipping whitespace |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `non_numeric_first_token_yields_zero` | matching failure on the first `scanf` |
